   <exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A general Sturm-Liouville problem is written in terms of
      functions <m>p(x)</m>, <m>q(x)</m> and <m>\sigma(x)</m> as
      <md>
        \frac{d}{d x}\left[p(x)\frac{d u}{d x}\right] + q(x)u = -k\sigma(x)u
      </md>
      along with some boundary conditions.  We want to understand
      eigenvalues <m>k</m> and nontrivial eigenfunctions <m>u(x)</m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Our most familiar Sturm-Liouville problem is
        <m>\frac{d^2u}{d x^2} = -ku</m>.  For this example, identify
        <m>p(x)</m>, <m>q(x)</m>, and <m>\sigma(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Bessel's equation of order <m>0</m>,
        (<m>x^2y''+xy'+x^2y=0</m>) rearranged and divided by <m>x</m> becomes
        <md>
          <mrow>x\frac{d^2u}{d x^2} + \frac{d u}{d x} = -kxu,</mrow>
        </md>
        which is also a Sturm-Liouville problem.
        Identify <m>p(x)</m>, <m>q(x)</m>, and <m>\sigma(x)</m> in this case.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a general Sturm-Liouville problem, we will use the shorthand
        <md>
          <mrow>L(u) = \frac{d}{d x}\left[p(x)\frac{d u}{d x}\right] + q(x)u.</mrow>
        </md>
        So <m>L</m> is an operation applied to <m>u</m>.  Verify Lagrange's
        identity:
        <md>
          <mrow>uL(v) - vL(u) =
          \frac{d}{d x}\left[p\left(u\frac{d v}{d x}-v\frac{d u}{d x}\right)\right].</mrow>
        </md>
        Hint:
        For <m>L(v)</m>, replace <m>u</m> with <m>v</m> in <m>L(u)</m>,
        work on both sides, and don't forget the product rule.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use this to verify Green's formula:
        <md>
          <mrow>\int_a^b\left[uL(v) - vL(u)\right]\,dx =\left.
          p\left(u\frac{d v}{d x}-v\frac{d u}{d x}\right)\right|_a^b.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

   <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use Green's formula to verify that eigenfunctions having
        different eigenvalues are orthogonal with weight <m>\sigma</m>.
        That is, suppose that
        <md>
          <mrow>L(u) \amp = -k\sigma(x) u(x)</mrow>
          <mrow>L(v) \amp = -l \sigma(x) v(x),</mrow>
        </md>
        where <m>k\neq l</m>, so <m>k</m> and <m>l</m> are the different
        eigenvalues. Suppose that we have also imposed boundary conditions,
        such as
        <md>
          <mrow>u(a) \amp = 0, \qquad u(b) = 0</mrow>
          <mrow>v(a) \amp = 0, \qquad v(b) = 0.</mrow>
        </md>
        We need to show that
        <m>\int_a^b u(x)v(x)\sigma(x)\,dx = 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        To do this, begin with Green's formula, and note that
        <md>
          <mrow>\int_a^b -vL(u)\,dx = \int_a^b -L(u)v(x)\,dx = \int_a^b ku(x)v(x)\sigma(x)\,dx.</mrow>
        </md>
        What else can we say using Green's formula?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the solution to Poisson's equation
      <md>
        <mrow>\nabla^2 u = y(1-y)x^3</mrow>
      </md>
      in the unit square <m>0\leq x \leq 1</m>, <m>0\leq y \leq 1</m>
      where <m>u=0</m> on the four sides.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The equation
      <md>
        <mrow>y''-2xy'+\lambda y=0,</mrow>
      </md>
      where <m>\lambda</m> is a constant, is known as the Hermite equation and
      is important in mathematical physics.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the first four terms in each of two linearly independent
        solutions.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Notice that if <m>\lambda</m> is a nonnegative even integer, then one
        of the solutions terminates and becomes a polynomial.  Find the
        polynomial solutions for <m>\lambda=0,2,4</m>, and <m>6</m>.  (Note
        that the polynomial is determined only up to a multiplicative
        constant.  The Hermite polynomial <m>H_n(x)</m> is defined to be the
        polynomial solution of the Hermite equation with <m>\lambda=2n</m> for
        which the coefficient of <m>x^n</m> is <m>2^n</m>.)
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find a solution to the Bessel equation of order two:
      <md>
        <mrow>x^2y'' + xy' + (x^2-4)y = 0</mrow>
      </md>
      that is bounded at the origin.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Solve the wave equation
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} = 9\nabla^2u</mrow>
      </md>
      for the circular membrane of radius 6.  The membrane is clamped on the
      boundary of the disk so that <m>u=0</m> on the boundary.  Let the initial
      velocity be 0
      (i.e. <m>\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}(r,\theta,0) = 0</m>),
      and the initial position be given by
      <md>
        <mrow>u(r,\theta,0) = -5J_0\left(\frac{z_{0,3}r}{6}\right) + 2 J_2\left(\frac{z_{2,3}r}{6}\right)\cos(2\theta)</mrow>
      </md>
      where <m>z_{n,m}</m> is the <m>m^{th}</m> zero of <m>J_n(x)</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>
    
</worksheet>